+# Flight 77 timing in the January presentation and May DOJ questions
+
+November 8, 2026 Eastern · TEL01–TEL04 / AUD016-A1 / TC05–TC06. Recovered two archival scans from the public Commission ZIP mirror. A January 25 staff presentation places the start of the Flight 77 calls at **9:18**. A May 14 list still asks DOJ to clarify their timing and content and seeks a transcript or tape of the attributed operator call. The documents give specific records to look for in addition to the January draft questions. [NARA finding aid, physical p. 109](https://www.archives.gov/files/research/9-11/finding-aid.pdf#page=109) · [Exact archive members and hashes](SOURCE-PINS.json) · [January question packet](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/5db40976a2dcc38d98a54ca3108ce3de6dee381d/investigation/broader-evidence/telephone-fbi-briefing-qfr-2026-11-08/README.md)
+
+## Timing and attribution
+
+| Record | Reading | Scope |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **January 25, 2004, Team 7 presentation to Commissioners**, Box 20, pp. 1, 2 and 6 | P. 1 lines 4–7 calls the briefing **“partial and preliminary.”** P. 2 item 3.J, lines 32–33, says **“Barbara Olson calls began at 9:18”** and summarizes reported content. P. 6 line 27 gives **09:18 / Barbara Olson call** in the Flight 77 column. | These are two displays in one briefing. The cited passages have minute precision and leave the underlying call record, clock and connection status unspecified. |
+| **May 14, 2004, list of questions for DOJ**, Box 21 item 264, p. 1 | Question 5, lines 29–30, requests clarification of timing and content. Question 6, lines 32–33, describes an **AT &T airphone operator** and asks whether the caller reported guns. Both have visible check marks. | The checked questions preserve what staff wanted answered. The marks have no identified author or meaning in the scan. The questions leave the answer and underlying evidence open. |
+| **May 20, 2004, DOJ briefing memo**, Box 12, p. 2 | Lines 7–21 show **9:18:58 / No Connection**, **9:25:48 / 159 seconds** and the briefers' reasoning for attributing unknown rows to the same caller despite their stated lack of direct evidence for those rows. P. 1 discusses **eight** calls, while p. 2 displays **seven** time rows. | The January minute label and May second-level status remain separate. Keep the later court zero-duration entry at **09:18:58** and the final report's **154 seconds** alongside these observations. An event key and field definitions are needed to test any proposed match. |
+
+The May 14 list also asks about the status of three listed Flight 175 calls to United's maintenance office, while recounting a one-call operator account. It asks whether the **9:35 a.m.** Flight 93 maintenance call went through in light of a reported one-call count. These are questions about attempts, connections and recipients. Keep both scopes visible pending the source rows and office logs. [Item 264, p. 1 lines 17–27](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+## What the audio and notes requests add
+
+- **April 8 Concordance memo, Box 21 item 267, pp. 1–2.** The held scan gives **4/8/04** on p. 1 line 8 and **4/6/2004** on p. 2 line 14. Its p. 1 lines 39–43 record a staff follow-up about whether the operator worked airphone or cellular service and what she recalled hearing. Its p. 2 lines 4–5 explicitly ask whether any AT&T and GTE lines were taped. The p. 2 search note says entering Flight 11 returned Flight 175. The scan preserves a reported search behavior and its separate dates, with no native query log.
+- **May 14 item 264, p. 2 question 10, lines 1–22.** Staff list eight audio entries, including the attributed Flight 77 call to an AT&T operator. The Flight 93 Lyles voicemail appears in two bullets. Only the latter carries **“COMISSION HAS TRANSCRIPT”** on the scan. The note excludes Flight 11 Ong and Sweeney materials from this request based on what staff believed was a complete airline set. The eight bullets are document entries with an apparent repeat. The inventory of delivered files, recording provenance and relationship between the two Lyles entries remain unverified.
+- **May 14 item 264, p. 2 question 11, lines 24–36.** Staff separately seek contemporaneous notes and written statements from recipients, expressly excluding FBI 302s from that question. This identifies a separate layer of records to look for alongside the interviews.
+- **May 20 briefing memo, pp. 1–2.** The memo describes the briefers' use of phone records, passenger cellular records and interviews, and sets out the displayed timing rows. It also discusses the press account and boarding-time precision, topics in the May 14 questions. The content overlap supplies a comparison lead. The memo does not record fulfillment of the May 14 tape or notes requests. [May 20 source review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/47b5e098fb7ae161ffea0b613ae3b17fba7dad55/investigation/broader-evidence/telephone-airline-reply-and-starfix-statements-2026-11-06/README.md)
+
+The April question about whether lines were taped and the May list's placement of the Flight 77 operator call among taped communications are both preserved. Which agency or carrier held a recording or transcript, whether a recording existed, and what was delivered remain open. The sources leave separate gaps between staff attribution, the operator's account, native switching events and any recording. [Earlier worksheet comparison](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/5031c6dfa73e1f59491f259e00f168c37cb21788/investigation/broader-evidence/telephone-usage-report-2026-10-27/README.md)
+
+## Deferred inputs
+
+- **TEL01–TEL02.** Find the final transmitted May 14 questions, cover message or intake, any written response, and the May 20 briefing exhibits or recording-delivery log. Check the tape/transcript inventory against questions 10 and 11 item by item. The January question dispatch and the exact PENTTBOM edition cited there remain open.
+- **TEL03–TEL04.** Recover the operator interview and native records tying it to an Airfone or other service and specific carrier/operator events. Compare unique call IDs, attempt and connection fields, clocks, and recipients before joining the January 9:18 label to the May rows or the court presentation. Recover any surviving contemporaneous notes, transcript or audio together with custody and transfer records.
+
+Both new scans passed the exact-member, HTTP range, validator, byte-count, CRC-32 and SHA-256 checks. Poppler text was screened across all eight and three pages respectively, with cited January pp. 1/2/6 and May pp. 1/2 regions checked on renderings. Two held comparison PDFs matched their stored hashes. The documentary check counted eight request bullets, two Lyles entries and seven May 20 time rows from the pinned PDFs. The raw telephone values and family/contact fields remain in their source records. No original switch record, tape, dispatch receipt or independently calibrated event time was acquired or tested in this unit. [Source pins and reproducible check scope](SOURCE-PINS.json)
